# Feature Implementation Guidelines

**glob**: `**/*.{jsx,tsx,js,ts}`

## Planning Phase

- Begin with a clear feature specification or user story
- Define acceptance criteria before starting development
- Break down complex features into smaller, manageable tasks
- Estimate time requirements for each task
- Identify potential dependencies on other components or services
- Review existing components that might be reused or extended
- Discuss the feature with team members to gather insights

## Development Workflow

- Create a feature branch from the main development branch
- Follow the naming convention: `feature/feature-name` or `feature/issue-number-feature-name`
- Make regular, atomic commits with descriptive messages
- Keep PRs manageable in size (preferably under 500 lines of code)
- Update documentation alongside code changes
- Implement features incrementally, starting with core functionality
- Use feature flags for controlled rollout when appropriate

## Component Implementation

- Create new components in the appropriate directory based on their purpose
- Follow the established project structure and organization
- Ensure components are reusable and composable where possible
- Extract business logic into custom hooks or utility functions
- Implement proper prop validation and TypeScript interfaces
- Ensure components render efficiently (avoid unnecessary re-renders)
- Add appropriate JSDoc comments for complex logic

## State Management

- Identify the appropriate scope for state (local vs. global)
- For local state, use React hooks (useState, useReducer)
- For global state, follow existing patterns (Redux, Context API, etc.)
- Keep state normalized and avoid duplication
- Implement proper error handling for async operations
- Consider optimistic UI updates for better user experience
- Add appropriate loading states for async operations

## API Integration

- Use existing API utilities and patterns for consistency
- Implement proper error handling and loading states
- Consider retry mechanisms for unstable network conditions
- Cache responses when appropriate to improve performance
- Validate API responses against expected schemas
- Write descriptive error messages for debugging purposes

## Responsive Design and Accessibility

- Ensure the feature works on all supported screen sizes
- Follow responsive design principles from the start
- Meet WCAG 2.1 AA accessibility standards
- Test with keyboard navigation
- Ensure proper color contrast and text readability
- Use semantic HTML elements and appropriate ARIA attributes
- Test with screen readers when implementing complex UI

## Testing Requirements

- Write unit tests for all new components and utilities
- Implement integration tests for complex interactions
- Add end-to-end tests for critical user journeys
- Include edge cases and error scenarios in test coverage
- Aim for test coverage of at least 80% for new code
- Test across different browsers if required by project standards
- Utilize test-driven development (TDD) when appropriate

## Performance Considerations

- Optimize component rendering (React.memo, useMemo, useCallback)
- Implement code splitting for large feature components
- Minimize bundle size by avoiding unnecessary dependencies
- Consider lazy loading for non-critical components
- Optimize images and assets used in the feature
- Implement appropriate caching strategies
- Monitor and benchmark performance during development

## Code Review Checklist

- Adherence to project coding standards and patterns
- No duplicate or unnecessary code
- Proper error handling and edge case management
- Adequate test coverage
- Responsive design implementation
- Accessibility compliance
- Performance considerations
- Security best practices
- Proper documentation

## Deployment Procedures

- Complete all items in the code review checklist
- Verify the feature works in all supported environments
- Test the feature in a staging environment
- Prepare release notes documenting the feature
- Plan rollback strategy in case of issues
- Consider gradual rollout using feature flags
- Monitor application metrics after deployment

## Documentation

- Update technical documentation reflecting the new feature
- Document any API changes or new endpoints
- Update user documentation or help resources
- Include screenshots or videos for visual features
- Document any configuration or environment requirements
- Add comments for complex logic or algorithms 